schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: Impact DAO
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A DAO (Decentralized Autonomous Organization) whose shared mission is
    specifically to create positive change — restoring ecosystems, funding
    local projects, or building alternative economic systems — rather than a
    DAO organized around, e.g., a protocol, an investment fund, or a social
    club.
  source_traditions:
    - General DAO organizing model (blockchain-coordinated, code-encoded rules)
    - Regenerative Finance (ReFi) movement
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Used in this article as the umbrella term for ReFi DAO, Greenpill, and
    Klima DAO, positioned as the web3-native analogue to existing
    cooperative/commons organizing (permaculture design courses, seed swaps,
    tool libraries) that a non-crypto-native reader already recognizes.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - >-
      "DAO" generally (any blockchain-coordinated group; not all DAOs are
      impact-oriented — see investment DAOs, protocol-governance DAOs)
    - Cooperative / commons organizing (non-blockchain analogue named explicitly
      in the article)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      Impact DAO vs. DAO broadly: the "impact" qualifier is a stated mission
      orientation, not a technical or legal distinction — nothing in the
      tooling enforces that a self-labeled Impact DAO actually delivers
      impact.
  tensions:
    - >-
      The article's own closing line ("Impact DAOs are not a magic solution...
      some will succeed, others will fail") sits in tension with the earlier
      framing of DAOs as trust-built-into-the-system; worth holding both.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Equating "no central boss" with genuine equity of voice risks flattening
    real participation and accountability challenges that DAOs face (named
    only briefly, in the related ethereum-ecosystem article, not here).
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/examples-impact-daos.md
    transformation: synthesized
    authorship: ai-assisted
